{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development would result in inadequate living conditions to prospective occupants on the site, by having insufficient storage space, which is contrary to the following planning policies:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- Technical Housing Standards (Nationally Described Space Standard) (as amended) (DCLG, March 2015);&lt;br&gt;- Policy D6 of the London Plan (March 2021);&lt;br&gt;- London Plan Guidance: Housing Design Standards (June 2023).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development, by reason of siting, design and appearance, would create an increased sense of closure. It would also unbalance the symmetry and disrupt the built form of the semi-detached pair and consequently constitute harm to the street scene and local character. As such, the proposal is contrary to:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;-&amp;nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, 2024);&lt;br&gt;- Policy D4 of the London Plan (June 2021);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;Policies SP2 and DMD1 of the London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development, by reason of siting, scale and appearance, would result in detrimental harm to the neighbouring property. Specifically, the proposed development would result in unacceptable overshadowing and overbearing impacts on 116 and 118 Charlton Crescent, therefore constituting unneighbourly development and contrary to:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;- National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, 2024);&lt;br&gt;- Policy D4 of the London Plan (March 2021);&lt;br&gt;- Policies SP2 and DMD1 of the London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposed development, by reasons of being situated in a PTAL 1b area and not being able to provide sufficient car parking space, would potentially increase parking pressure to the surroundings, which is considered unacceptable from the perspective of transport/highway, therefore contrary to the following policies:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;-&amp;nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, 2024);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;Policy T6 of the London Plan (March 2021);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;London Plan Guidance: Housing Design Standards (GLA, June 2023);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;Policy DMT2 of the&amp;nbsp;London Borough of Barking and Dagenham’s Local Plan (September 2024).&lt;/p&gt;",
    "&lt;p&gt;The proposals do not have sufficient information to prove that it would meet the requirement to have a 10% of the Biodiversity Net Gain. As such, officers are unable to conclude that the propose development would offer ecological improvements to the site and the surrounding area. As such, it is considered contrary to:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;-&amp;nbsp;Schedule 7A of the Town and Country Planning Act 1990 (as inserted by Schedule 14 of the Environment Act 2021, April 2024);&lt;br&gt;-&amp;nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (NPPF) (DLUHC, 2024);&lt;br&gt;- Policies G5 and G6 of the London Plan (March 2021);&lt;br&gt;- Policy&amp;nbsp;DMNE3 of the LBBD&#x27;s Local Plan (September, 2024)&lt;/p&gt;"
  ],
